WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.840
In 1991, Geoff Crammond and MicroProse shipped Formula One Grand Prix.

2
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:09.437
Twenty-six cars.

3
00:00:09.437 --> 00:00:11.055
Sixteen real circuits.

4
00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:14.364
A filled-polygon 3D world, on an ordinary PC.

5
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:18.550
This is a look inside how it works, and how it was brought back to life.

6
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.604
The game takes over the whole machine.

7
00:00:22.604 --> 00:00:27.936
It is a sixteen-bit DOS program, with no operating system to help once a race begins.

8
00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:31.843
It speeds the PC's timer up to three hundred interrupts a second.

9
00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:34.759
Every sixth one, the whole world takes a step.

10
00:00:34.759 --> 00:00:38.068
Fifty ticks a second, and everything keeps time to that beat.

11
00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:42.215
A circuit is not stored as a 3D model.

12
00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:45.144
Each track file is a list of instructions.

13
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:45.861
Go this far.

14
00:00:45.861 --> 00:00:46.757
Bend this much.

15
00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:47.115
Climb.

16
00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:48.071
Put a wall here.

17
00:00:48.491 --> 00:00:55.819
When a race loads, the game runs those instructions, and lays down a forty-six-byte record for every sixteen feet of tarmac.

18
00:00:57.488 --> 00:01:02.028
Every car, yours included, is one hundred and ninety-two bytes in a single table.

19
00:01:02.448 --> 00:01:06.986
Thirty-four entrants become twenty-six, through a real pre-qualifying session.

20
00:01:07.406 --> 00:01:10.903
And the computer drivers use exactly the same physics as you.

21
00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.737
Only their inputs are different.

22
00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:20.610
Every frame, the game paints a picture three hundred and twenty pixels wide, using only whole-number arithmetic.

23
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.825
Sky first, then road, kerbs, trees and cars, from back to front,

24
00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:29.524
all seen through the windows of a cockpit it keeps inside the program itself.

25
00:01:31.194 --> 00:01:34.224
The sound comes from one small Yamaha FM chip.

26
00:01:34.644 --> 00:01:36.755
The engine note is not a recording.

27
00:01:36.755 --> 00:01:41.460
It is three chip voices, retuned from the engine's revs, fifty times a second.

28
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.070
Two PCs could even race each other, joined by a serial cable,

29
00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:49.800
or by modem, down a telephone line.

30
00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:55.679
Thirty-five years later, a small project set out to make it run again.

31
00:01:55.679 --> 00:01:56.160
Exactly.

32
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.641
Twenty-four days.

33
00:01:57.641 --> 00:02:02.320
Two thousand six hundred commits, most of them written by AI coding agents.

34
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.618
The result is called Big Race.

35
00:02:04.618 --> 00:02:08.187
The original's routines, re-expressed one by one in Rust.

36
00:02:09.858 --> 00:02:13.979
The key was an oracle: the original game, running inside an emulator.

37
00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:18.232
Every ported routine was checked against it, byte for byte.

38
00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:20.985
The bar was never, looks like the game.

39
00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:23.739
It was: writes the same bytes the game writes.

40
00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.186
Wrong turns were written down too.

41
00:02:27.186 --> 00:02:31.520
Like the routine long believed to be the physics, which turned out to build tracks.

42
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.165
And tests that were green, but proved nothing.

43
00:02:35.835 --> 00:02:39.396
Big Race drops into your own copy of the game, and bundles none of it.

44
00:02:39.816 --> 00:02:45.801
It adds sharp modern graphics, video recording, and races across a home network, carried as modem sound.

45
00:02:47.472 --> 00:02:53.046
Part one takes the 1991 game apart, from the timer to the menus and saves.

46
00:02:53.046 --> 00:02:55.983
Part two tells how Big Race rebuilt it.

47
00:02:56.403 --> 00:03:02.717
It is an unofficial project, and it is all written up, chapter by chapter, in Inside Formula One Grand Prix.
